case: c_set_directions
ac: AC-33 (c), reading per SPEC 5.0: several elements are sent as several ops, each checked on its own (G12)
kind: ops
compile: ostrel check exits 0 with exactly one warning, on line 14 (`edit watchers ...`), saying
  that the rule does not mention removals, so every removal from `watchers` is denied (AC-55);
  its code and text are assigned by the v0.2 checker task
principals: alice, bob, carol, dave (development identities, debug build, AC-12)
given: alice has made Project p1 { name: "Launch" }, so p1.author = alice and p1.watchers = {alice}
steps:
  1. bob sends one op: add bob to p1.watchers
     rule: added = {bob}, removed = {}; added == {me} is true
     expect: Ack; watchers = {alice, bob}
  2. carol sends one op: add dave to p1.watchers
     rule: author == me is false, added == {me} is false
     expect: Reject Denied; watchers = {alice, bob}
  3. alice replaces bob by dave, sent as two ops in two separate batches:
     3a. add dave to p1.watchers
         rule: author == me is true, direction added
         expect: Ack; watchers = {alice, bob, dave}
     3b. remove bob from p1.watchers
         rule: the expression `author == me or added == {me}` is true for alice, but the rule
         does not mention removals
         expect: Reject Denied; watchers = {alice, bob, dave}
  4. bob sends one op: remove bob from p1.watchers (leave)
     expect: Reject Denied; watchers = {alice, bob, dave}
final db: p1.watchers = {alice, bob, dave}; no removal of any element is stored in the op log or
  in the side table of `watchers`
must not: step 3b or step 4 accepted; a removal evaluated as allowed because the expression is
  true for its principal
not covered here: one wire op that carries more than one element or both directions (Invalid,
  AC-55); whether ops in one batch are accepted together is not assumed, so step 3 uses two batches
